WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.150
Pastry: crisp shortcrust, flaky rough puff, and hollow choux.

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.640
This is the video for module three of Advanced Baking and Pastry.

3
00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:16.440
Pastry rewards precise temperature control. Shortcrust snaps because cold fat shortens the gluten.

4
00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:21.180
Rough puff rises as steam pushes thin layers apart.

5
00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:27.450
Choux has no yeast or baking powder. Steam alone blows it into a hollow shell.

6
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.000
And every fault has a cause you can find.

7
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:39.460
You know shortcrust from meat pie. The ratio is 2 parts flour to 1 part cold butter, with a pinch of salt.

8
00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:51.020
Rub the butter in quickly with cool fingertips until it looks like fine breadcrumbs. Add cold water a little at a time, and stop as soon as a handful holds together.

9
00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:57.960
Wrap and chill at least 30 minutes to relax the gluten, then roll to about 3 millimetres.

10
00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:04.570
Ease the pastry down into the corners. Stretched pastry pulls back and shrinks in the oven.

11
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:21.370
Blind bake the empty case with paper and baking beans, at about 190 to 200 degrees for roughly 15 minutes. Remove the beans, bake 5 to 10 minutes more, then brush with egg and bake 2 minutes to seal it under wet fillings.

12
00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:28.250
In a hot kitchen, chill soft dough rather than adding more flour, which toughens the pastry.

13
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:37.420
Use equal weights of flour and cold butter. Toss the butter cubes in the flour, but do not rub them in. You want whole lumps.

14
00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:43.530
Add ice-cold water to make a rough dough with visible butter, then chill 20 minutes.

15
00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:48.190
Roll a long rectangle and fold it in three, like a letter.

16
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:57.340
Turn it a quarter turn and repeat. If the butter looks soft or shiny, chill it. Melted butter gives tough, greasy pastry.

17
00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:11.900
Each fold triples the layers: 3, then 9, 27, and 81 after four folds. Cut with a sharp knife, and bake at about 200 to 220 degrees so steam forms fast.

18
00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:20.020
Bring the water, milk and butter to a full boil. Off the heat, tip in all the flour and beat hard until smooth.

19
00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:28.500
Stir on medium heat for 1 to 2 minutes, until a thin film forms on the pan bottom. This dries the paste.

20
00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.930
Beat it in a bowl until just warm.

21
00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:45.400
Add egg a little at a time. Eggs vary, so never add a fixed amount. Stop when the paste falls slowly from the spatula and leaves a V-shaped flag. Too much egg, and the shells spread flat.

22
00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:58.060
Bake at about 200 degrees for 15 minutes, then about 180 for 15 to 20 more, without opening the door. Pierce each shell and dry it in the switched-off oven.

23
00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:12.540
Egg and milk custards are ideal places for bacteria to grow. Pastry cream must bubble for a full 1 to 2 minutes. Cool it quickly in a shallow dish, keep it in the fridge, and use it within about 2 days.

24
00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:18.140
Fill choux as close to serving as possible, and keep filled pastries chilled.

25
00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:32.640
Cook meat fillings until the centre is piping hot and no longer pink. Keep raw meat and its boards and knives away from finished food. And anything with a food safety doubt must be thrown away, never sold.

26
00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:41.530
For example, Rukayat in Ilorin had a tray of custard tarts returned. The bases were pale and wet underneath.

27
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:51.500
Her batch record showed the usual 200 grams of flour, 100 of butter and 45 millilitres of water. No change.

28
00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.270
The dough was rested and chilled as usual.

29
00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:06.230
But she had skipped the egg seal and baked on the bottom shelf, which her oven card says runs cooler. She fixed both, and the next bases were crisp and golden.

30
00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:16.860
Write down weights, room temperature, chilling, oven setting, shelf and time for every batch. Change only one thing at a time when you test a fix.

31
00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:28.120
To remember. Keep shortcrust cold and ease it into the tin. Give rough puff four letter folds and keep the butter cold. Dry the choux paste and use the V test.

32
00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:34.680
Chill custard fillings fast. And find faults in order: ratio, temperature, handling, then baking.

33
00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:42.840
Now blind bake six tart cases, laminate and compare, make a profiterole batch, and start your batch record.

34
00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:45.130
Continue with the lessons below.
